A1. Freethinkers. ~ BACHELORS
A9. People have been requested not to tip this one. ~ CANOE
A10. Old-style pitchers. ~ EWERS
A12. Accessories for girders. ~ LOINS
A13. Dispatched. ~ SENT
A14. An afternoon affair. (abbr.) ~ PM
A16. The arm the soldier lost in the war. ~ SABRE
A17. A whoopee time. ~ SPREE
A18. A sheepish father. ~ RAM
A20. This is what a worm always does. ~ TURNS
A21. Sits up and takes notice. ~ HEEDS
A22. Another form of being. ~ ARE
A23. A crossword-puzzle sweetheart. ~ ADA
A24. This is what you will have to do to the furnace this winter. ~ FEED
A25. Sometimes these get in wrong. ~ INS
A26. This should be overlooked by the editor. (abbr.) ~ MS
A27. Oil well. (As Pat would say.) ~ CURED
A29. This is what she made after her partner trumped her ace. (plur.) ~ SCENES
A32. How the man at the pound puts out the cat. ~ CHLOROFORMS
A34. What some ball players are doing. ~ ERRING
A36. The Persians relatives (anc. hist.) ~ MEDES
A37. A printer's measure. ~ EN
A38. The goddess that made the moonshine. ~ DIANA
A39. Where you find the under dog. ~ BELOW
A40. To hang down. ~ SAG
A41. An urger. ~ EGGER
A42. Small matters. ~ ATOMS
A43. This is what a Scotchman will do to an old razor blade. ~ USE
A44. This is what started the golden rule. ~ DO
A45. This tip came from a Jap. ~ SEN
A46. This is the way curious people look. ~ PEER
A47. A cock-eyed daddy. ~ ROOSTER
A52. These are always eating something. ~ ACIDS
A54. These go in leaps and bounds. ~ FLEAS
A55. The kind of a man who waits for his wife. ~ IRATE
A56. The profession that gets on one's nerves. ~ DENTISTRY

D1. A small Scotchman. ~ BAIRN
D2. Something that belonged to Henry VIII's second wife. ~ ANNES
D3. Big get-togethers. (abbr.) ~ COS
D4. The kind of a goat Billy is. ~ HE
D5. This is the way the villain looked. ~ LEERED
D6. This is what dad did after he made his last payment. ~ OWNED
D7. Networks that display a lot of nerve. ~ RETES
D8. This denotes age. (abbr.) ~ SR
D9. Don't let this one get his fangs on you. ~ COBRA
D11. Wrenches that motorists sometimes carry. ~ SPRAINS
D12. A good critic never does this. ~ LAUDS
D13. This is the fastest thing of the times. ~ SPEEDOMETER
D15. This is where the Germans lost out. ~ MARNE
D16. This is what they did to raise the dust on the Western plains. ~ STAMPEDED
D17. It isn't hard to see through this kind of a thing. ~ SHEER
D19. This one is always taking candy and flowers to some other fellow's sweetie. ~ MESSENGER
D24. This one is always putting up a kick in a pool game. ~ FROG
D27. The backbone of a pig. ~ CHINE
D28. Pertaining to the ulna. ~ ULNAR
D29. This is where a Lot turned to rubber. ~ SODOM
D30. Gangs. ~ CREWS
D31. These need more space to park than Hor. 37. ~ EMS
D32. This is a rough one. ~ CRAG
D33. A pen pal. ~ FELON
D35. Severity. ~ RIGOR
D37. What wealth does to the lives of the younger generation. ~ EASES
D39. This is the worst yet. ~ BASEST
D40. This one has no polish. ~ SUEDE
D46. They say this is akin to love. ~ PITY
D48. A preposition. ~ OF
D49. These people never die young. ~ OLD
D50. What an Englishman tries to do with a monocle. ~ SEE
D51. A sunshade. ~ TAN
D52. You see a lot of this in Paris. ~ ART
D53. Now you can get this smaller than you can af-ford. ~ CAR
D55. Exists. ~ IS


